1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,597
This is STAT 331, video 4. The topic is least squares estimation of the simple linear regression line.

2
00:00:09,047 --> 00:00:15,511
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:15,961 --> 00:00:25,284
Type A: derive the least squares estimators. Lecture 2 does it, and Tutorial 2, question 2 b, asks it for a special x.

4
00:00:25,734 --> 00:00:34,118
Type B: compute beta zero hat and beta one hat from a data table or from computer output, as in Tutorial 1.

5
00:00:34,568 --> 00:00:40,136
Type C: interpret beta one hat and beta zero hat in the units of the problem.

6
00:00:40,586 --> 00:00:54,730
Here is the model from the last video. y i equals beta zero plus beta one x i plus epsilon i. The errors are independent and normal, with mean 0 and variance sigma squared.

7
00:00:55,180 --> 00:01:09,943
These are the data of Tutorial 1, question 1. x is the number of machine hours in a month. y is the maintenance cost, in hundreds of dollars. There are 7 months. Each column of the table is one point.

8
00:01:10,393 --> 00:01:14,275
We can draw many lines through these points. Line A is flat.

9
00:01:14,725 --> 00:01:16,197
Line B is steep.

10
00:01:16,647 --> 00:01:20,487
Line C looks better than A and B. But looks are not a rule.

11
00:01:20,937 --> 00:01:35,167
Beta zero and beta one are fixed, unknown parameters. We need a rule that uses the data to pick one line. Slide 18 asks this question: how do we estimate beta zero and beta one?

12
00:01:35,617 --> 00:01:42,251
Take one candidate line, beta zero plus beta one x. The gray line is one choice.

13
00:01:42,701 --> 00:01:49,699
Look at one point, x i, y i. Here it is the month with 88 hours and a cost of 40.

14
00:01:50,149 --> 00:01:57,295
At the same x, the line gives the value beta zero plus beta one x i. The white dot shows it.

15
00:01:57,745 --> 00:02:06,535
The vertical distance between the two is y i minus beta zero minus beta one x i. The orange segment shows it.

16
00:02:06,985 --> 00:02:12,915
Each point has one such distance. A good line makes all these distances small together.

17
00:02:13,365 --> 00:02:20,363
We use vertical distances, because the model puts the error epsilon i on y, not on x.

18
00:02:20,813 --> 00:02:37,090
Here is the flat line at y equals 27. 27 is y bar: the costs add to 189, and 189 over 7 is 27. The labels show the seven signed vertical distances.

19
00:02:37,540 --> 00:02:46,991
Add the signed distances: minus 1, minus 2, minus 7, minus 9, plus 3, plus 13, plus 3. The sum is 0.

20
00:02:47,441 --> 00:03:04,891
The positive and the negative distances cancel. The purple line also goes through the mean point, 101, 27, and its signed sum is also 0. Every line through the mean point has sum 0. So this sum cannot pick one line.

21
00:03:05,341 --> 00:03:19,613
So square each distance first. Every square is positive or zero, so nothing cancels. Large distances count more than small ones. The sum of the squares is S of beta zero and beta one.

22
00:03:20,063 --> 00:03:23,071
Least squares picks the line with the smallest S.

23
00:03:23,521 --> 00:03:34,209
On the left are the Tutorial 1 data. Every line on this slide goes through the mean point, in purple. On the right, we plot S against the slope of the line.

24
00:03:34,659 --> 00:03:49,763
First, the flat line, slope 0. Each vertical distance becomes a real square. Its side is the distance, so its area is the squared distance. S is the total area of the squares: 322.

25
00:03:50,213 --> 00:03:56,976
Now a slope of minus 0.2. The squares shrink. S equals 161.

26
00:03:57,426 --> 00:04:04,978
A slope of minus 0.7 is too steep. The squares grow again. S equals 213.

27
00:04:05,428 --> 00:04:13,364
The best slope is minus 0.41. Its squares are the smallest. S equals 104.1.

28
00:04:13,814 --> 00:04:26,123
Here is S for every slope. The curve is a parabola, so it has one lowest point, at minus 0.41. At the lowest point, the curve is flat: its slope is zero.

29
00:04:26,573 --> 00:04:40,333
So least squares means: choose beta zero hat and beta one hat that make S smallest. At the minimum, both partial derivatives of S are zero. This gives two equations in two unknowns.

30
00:04:40,783 --> 00:04:55,226
Section 2. Question type A asks you to derive least squares estimators. Lecture 2 does it for the simple linear model. Tutorial 2, question 2 b, asks it for an x that is 0 or 1.

31
00:04:55,676 --> 00:05:06,044
Method card A has four steps. Step 1: write S, the sum of the squared vertical distances. Use the mean of y i that the model gives.

32
00:05:06,494 --> 00:05:15,603
Step 2: take the partial derivative of S with respect to each parameter. The orange line shows a derivative that is not 0.

33
00:05:16,053 --> 00:05:26,528
Step 3: set each derivative to 0, and put hats on the parameters. The hats mark the estimates. At the lowest point of S, the derivative is 0.

34
00:05:26,978 --> 00:05:33,719
Step 4: solve the equations for the hats. The solution, beta hat, is the lowest point of S.

35
00:05:34,169 --> 00:05:40,975
So steps 2 to 4 find the lowest point of S. Now we use the card on the slides of lecture 2.

36
00:05:41,425 --> 00:06:02,075
Here is the derivation of slide 23. We follow card A. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: write S. It is the sum, from i equals 1 to n, of y i minus beta zero minus beta one x i, squared. The picture shows the vertical distances to one line.

37
00:06:02,525 --> 00:06:22,045
Step 2: take the partial derivative with respect to beta zero. Use the chain rule on the underlined square. The outer derivative is 2 times the bracket. The inner derivative is minus 1, in orange. On the right, this derivative is the slope of S along beta zero.

38
00:06:22,495 --> 00:06:36,191
Step 3: set the derivative to 0. Divide both sides by minus 2. Put hats on the betas, because the solution is our estimate. On the right, the slope is 0 at the lowest point of S.

39
00:06:36,641 --> 00:06:58,252
Step 4: solve. Split the sum into three sums. The sum of beta zero hat, n times, is n beta zero hat. Beta one hat comes out of its sum. For the Tutorial 1 data, line 4 is 0 equals 189 minus 7 beta zero hat minus 707 beta one hat.

40
00:06:58,702 --> 00:07:18,414
Divide by n. The sum of y i over n is y bar. The sum of x i over n is x bar. So beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar. For the Tutorial 1 data, this is 27 minus 101 beta one hat.

41
00:07:18,864 --> 00:07:35,035
Move the terms: y bar equals beta zero hat plus beta one hat x bar. So the fitted line passes through the mean point, x bar, y bar. Each slope gives one line through this point. The next slide finds the slope.

42
00:07:35,485 --> 00:07:51,869
Now the slope, slide 25. Pause the video and try it. Step 2 of card A: take the partial derivative with respect to beta one. The chain rule again. This time the inner derivative is minus x i, in orange.

43
00:07:52,319 --> 00:07:56,564
Step 3: set it to 0, divide by minus 2, and put hats.

44
00:07:57,014 --> 00:08:13,867
Step 4: solve. Multiply out the bracket. The sum of x i is n x bar, so the middle term is beta zero hat times n x bar. In the table, the sum of x is 707, which is 7 times 101.

45
00:08:14,317 --> 00:08:31,064
Put in the result of slide 23: beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar. Now beta one hat is the only unknown. The plot shows S along the lines through the mean point, as a function of the slope.

46
00:08:31,514 --> 00:08:38,213
Multiply out the bracket. Minus times minus gives plus beta one hat n x bar squared.

47
00:08:38,663 --> 00:08:41,180
Collect the two terms with beta one hat.

48
00:08:41,630 --> 00:08:57,993
Solve for beta one hat. The numerator is the sum of y i x i minus n x bar y bar. The denominator is the sum of x i squared minus n x bar squared. This is the result on slide 25.

49
00:08:58,443 --> 00:09:37,733
Check with the Tutorial 1 data. The table gives the sum of x y, 18558, and the sum of x squared, 72701. The numerator is 18558 minus 19089, which is minus 531. The denominator is 72701 minus 71407, which is 1294. So beta one hat is minus 0.4104, the lowest point of S.

50
00:09:38,183 --> 00:09:48,017
The formula of slide 25 is long. Slide 26 makes it short. We are still in step 4 of card A: simplify the solution.

51
00:09:48,467 --> 00:10:08,179
n x bar y bar equals x bar times the sum of y i. And n x bar squared equals x bar times the sum of x i. So the top is the sum of y i times x i minus x bar, and the bottom is the sum of x i times x i minus x bar.

52
00:10:08,629 --> 00:10:23,584
A fact: the deviations x i minus x bar always add to 0. The picture shows the seven deviations of the Tutorial 1 hours. They add to 0. So y bar times their sum is 0.

53
00:10:24,034 --> 00:10:41,783
Subtract this zero on the top, and subtract x bar times the same zero on the bottom. The top becomes the sum of y i minus y bar times x i minus x bar. The bottom becomes the sum of x i minus x bar, squared.

54
00:10:42,233 --> 00:10:48,143
These two sums have names. The top is S x y. The bottom is S x x.

55
00:10:48,593 --> 00:11:03,739
The results: beta one hat equals S x y over S x x, and beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar. Question type B uses these two formulas in steps 3 and 4.

56
00:11:04,189 --> 00:11:29,875
Now a tutorial question of type A. Pause the video and try it. In Tutorial 2, question 2 b, x i is 0 for the first n 1 subjects, group 0, and 1 for the next n 2 subjects, group 1. Show that beta zero hat is y bar 0 and beta one hat is y bar 1 minus y bar 0. The picture uses example values.

57
00:11:30,325 --> 00:11:35,210
Step 1 of card A: write S. It is the usual simple linear model.

58
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:46,583
Steps 2 and 3: the partial derivative with respect to beta zero, set to 0. This is line 3 of slide 23, so we use it directly.

59
00:11:47,033 --> 00:11:59,939
The partial derivative with respect to beta one, set to 0. This is line 2 of slide 25. The two x i are underlined, because the next line uses their values.

60
00:12:00,389 --> 00:12:12,571
Step 4: solve. In line 3, x i is 0 in group 0, so those terms drop out. In group 1, x i is 1. So the sum runs over group 1 only.

61
00:12:13,021 --> 00:12:22,919
Divide by n 2. The mean of y in group 1 is y bar 1. So beta zero hat plus beta one hat equals y bar 1.

62
00:12:23,369 --> 00:12:38,431
Now split line 2 into the two groups. The group 1 part is 0, by line 4. So the group 0 part is 0 too. In group 0, x i is 0, so the term beta one hat x i drops out.

63
00:12:38,881 --> 00:12:45,622
Divide by n 1. So beta zero hat equals y bar 0, the mean of group 0.

64
00:12:46,072 --> 00:12:53,048
Put line 7 into line 5. Beta one hat equals y bar 1 minus y bar 0.

65
00:12:53,498 --> 00:13:03,055
The fitted line goes through the two group means. Beta one hat is the difference of the two means. This is the result that the question asks for.

66
00:13:03,505 --> 00:13:17,927
Section 3. Question type B asks you to compute the least squares estimates. The data come as a table, as in Tutorial 1, question 1, or as computer output, as in Tutorial 1, question 2.

67
00:13:18,377 --> 00:13:23,283
Step 1 of method card B: find the means, x bar and y bar.

68
00:13:23,733 --> 00:13:33,205
Step 2: find S x x and S x y. With a data table, use columns of deviations. With computer output, read them.

69
00:13:33,655 --> 00:13:38,349
Step 3: beta one hat equals S x y over S x x.

70
00:13:38,799 --> 00:13:44,409
Step 4: beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar.

71
00:13:44,859 --> 00:13:56,763
Step 5: check. Each column of deviations adds to 0. The sign of beta one hat is the sign of S x y. With output, compare with the printed estimates.

72
00:13:57,213 --> 00:14:10,803
Now a tutorial question of type B. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 1 a, gives seven months of machine hours and maintenance cost. Find the least squares estimates.

73
00:14:11,253 --> 00:14:27,487
Step 1 of card B: the means. The x values add to 707, so x bar is 707 over 7, which is 101. The y values add to 189, so y bar is 27.

74
00:14:27,937 --> 00:14:39,436
Step 2: the deviations from the means. In row 1, 110 minus 101 is plus 9, and 26 minus 27 is minus 1.

75
00:14:39,886 --> 00:14:46,713
Step 5 of the card is a check, and we can do it now: each deviation column adds to 0.

76
00:14:47,163 --> 00:14:58,491
Back to step 2. Square the x deviations. In row 1, 9 squared is 81. The sum is S x x, which is 1294.

77
00:14:58,941 --> 00:15:11,527
Multiply the two deviations in each row. In row 1, plus 9 times minus 1 is minus 9. The sum is S x y, which is minus 531.

78
00:15:11,977 --> 00:15:25,524
Step 3: beta one hat is S x y over S x x. Minus 531 over 1294 is minus 0.4104.

79
00:15:25,974 --> 00:15:43,723
Step 4: beta zero hat is y bar minus beta one hat x bar. 27 minus minus 0.4104 times 101 is 27 plus 41.45, which is 68.45.

80
00:15:44,173 --> 00:15:58,040
Step 5: S x y is negative, so beta one hat must be negative, and it is. The fitted line is y hat equals 68.45 minus 0.4104 x.

81
00:15:58,490 --> 00:16:07,471
Here is the reason for the sign check of step 5. Draw the mean lines. They cross at the mean point, 101, 27.

82
00:16:07,921 --> 00:16:25,287
Each product of deviations is the signed area of a rectangle. One corner is the mean point. The other corner is a data point. For x equals 88, the area is minus 13 times plus 13, which is minus 169.

83
00:16:25,737 --> 00:16:31,731
Points to the upper left or the lower right of the mean point give negative areas, in red.

84
00:16:32,181 --> 00:16:43,211
Points to the upper right or the lower left give positive areas, in blue. Here only one point, x equals 98, is lower left. Its area is plus 6.

85
00:16:43,661 --> 00:17:00,813
S x y is the total signed area: minus 531. S x x is a sum of squares, so it is always positive. So beta one hat has the same sign as S x y. More hours go with a lower cost in these data.

86
00:17:01,263 --> 00:17:15,876
Another type B question, now from computer output. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 2 a, gives R output for 100 aircraft. Verify the two estimates with our formulas.

87
00:17:16,326 --> 00:17:26,822
Step 1 of card B: read the means. x bar is 148.8 flight hours. y bar is 1822.

88
00:17:27,272 --> 00:17:44,616
Step 2: read S x x, 731093, and S x y, 4686071. The output gives them as the sums of squared deviations and of products of deviations.

89
00:17:45,066 --> 00:18:05,119
Step 3: beta one hat is S x y over S x x. 4686071 over 731093 is 6.4097, which rounds to 6.410. This matches the output.

90
00:18:05,569 --> 00:18:26,881
Step 4: beta zero hat is y bar minus beta one hat x bar. 6.4097 times 148.8 is 953.8. 1822 minus 953.8 is 868.2.

91
00:18:27,331 --> 00:18:37,422
Step 5: compare. The output says 868.487. The output prints x bar rounded to 148.8.

92
00:18:37,872 --> 00:18:56,283
With x bar equal to 148.761, which also rounds to 148.8, the formula gives 868.49. So the estimates agree. The small difference comes only from the rounding of x bar.

93
00:18:56,733 --> 00:19:04,050
Section 4. Question type C asks you to interpret the estimates. Method card C has four steps.

94
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:10,473
Step 1: write the units of x and of y. Every sentence of the answer uses them.

95
00:19:10,923 --> 00:19:30,358
Step 2: beta one hat. Slide 16 shows that beta one is the change in the mean of y when x increases by one unit. So: for each one-unit increase in x, the estimated mean of y changes by beta one hat. Say increase or decrease, from the sign.

96
00:19:30,808 --> 00:19:41,091
Step 3: beta zero hat is the estimated mean of y when x is 0. In the picture, it is the height of the line at x equals 0.

97
00:19:41,541 --> 00:19:51,525
Step 4: check the range of x in the data. If x equals 0 is far outside this range, beta zero hat has no practical meaning.

98
00:19:51,975 --> 00:20:09,831
A type C question. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 1 b: give an interpretation of beta one hat. From part a, the fitted line is y hat equals 68.45 minus 0.4104 x.

99
00:20:10,281 --> 00:20:19,433
Step 1 of card C: the units. x is machine hours per month. y is the monthly maintenance cost in hundreds of dollars.

100
00:20:19,883 --> 00:20:37,056
Step 2: beta one hat is negative, so the estimated mean cost decreases. The purple triangle shows 10 extra hours. The fitted mean cost drops by 10 times 0.4104, which is 4.10 hundred dollars.

101
00:20:37,506 --> 00:20:57,837
So the answer: for each additional machine hour per month, the estimated mean monthly maintenance cost decreases by 0.41 hundred dollars. 0.4104 times 100 dollars is 41.04 dollars, about 41 dollars.

102
00:20:58,287 --> 00:21:07,417
The marked parts are the four parts of a full answer: one more unit of x, the estimated mean of y, the direction, and the units.

103
00:21:07,867 --> 00:21:30,950
The lecture uses the Gladstone data from 1905. There are 236 people. Step 1 of card C: x is head size in cubic centimeters, and y is brain weight in grams. R gives beta zero hat 335.4323 and beta one hat 0.2608.

104
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:47,571
Step 2: for each extra cubic centimeter of head size, the estimated mean brain weight is 0.26 grams higher. The triangle shows 1000 extra cubic centimeters: 260.8 grams more.

105
00:21:48,021 --> 00:22:01,034
Step 3: beta zero hat, 335.4 grams, is the estimated mean brain weight at head size 0. It is the height of the line at x equals 0.

106
00:22:01,484 --> 00:22:18,764
Step 4: the head sizes in the data go from 2773 to 4747 cubic centimeters. Head size 0 is far outside this range. So beta zero hat has no practical meaning here.

107
00:22:19,214 --> 00:22:35,022
The last problem is from Assignment 1, question 3 b. Pause the video and try it. There are 90 berries. x is sugar content in grams per liter. y is chewiness in millijoules. Compute the estimates and interpret them.

108
00:22:35,472 --> 00:22:55,909
First the estimates, with card B. From the data file: x bar is 217.33, y bar is 2.708, S x x is 70657.4, and S x y is minus 1610.78.

109
00:22:56,359 --> 00:23:25,074
Beta one hat is minus 1610.78 over 70657.4, which is minus 0.0228. Beta zero hat is 2.708 plus 0.0228 times 217.33. That is 2.708 plus 4.955, which is 7.663. The green line is the fitted line.

110
00:23:25,524 --> 00:23:37,236
Now card C. Steps 1 and 2: for each additional gram per liter of sugar, the estimated mean chewiness decreases by 0.0228 millijoules.

111
00:23:37,686 --> 00:23:46,902
Step 3: beta zero hat, 7.663 millijoules, is the estimated mean chewiness at sugar content 0.

112
00:23:47,352 --> 00:24:04,013
Step 4: the sugar values in the data go from 176.5 to 258.5 grams per liter. Sugar 0 is far outside this range. So beta zero hat has no practical meaning here.

113
00:24:04,463 --> 00:24:13,082
Slide 26 ends with a question. Why do we minimize the squares, and not another function, such as the absolute distances?

114
00:24:13,532 --> 00:24:34,865
The answer uses the model. Each y i is normal, with mean beta zero plus beta one x i, and variance sigma squared. The orange curves show this normal density at three values of x. In the next video, maximum likelihood with this model gives the same estimates as least squares.

115
00:24:35,315 --> 00:24:59,294
To recap. Least squares picks the line with the smallest sum of squared vertical distances. Card A derives the estimators: write S, take the partial derivatives, set them to 0, and solve. The result is beta one hat equals S x y over S x x, and beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar.

116
00:24:59,744 --> 00:25:07,296
Card B computes the estimates: the means, S x x and S x y, the slope, the intercept, and a check.

117
00:25:07,746 --> 00:25:28,141
Card C interprets them: the units, the slope as the change in the estimated mean of y for one more unit of x, and the intercept only when x equals 0 is near the data. The next video covers maximum likelihood, fitted values, residuals, and the estimate of sigma squared.
